(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-ent-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Advanced ENT History & Examination Quiz 2 — PD2 Exam 1","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Besides the whispered voice, which two bedside hearing screens are used?","choices":["Weber and Rinne","Finger rub and a watch","Tuning fork and audiometry","Otoscopy and insufflation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — both are crude but immediately available, and both test whether any sound is heard at all. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is tinnitus, by definition?","choices":["Hearing loss confined to the high frequencies","A sensation of fullness or pressure in the ear","Sound perceived with no external source","A perception of rotation or spinning"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The definition turns on the absence of an external stimulus, which is what separates it from any complaint about external sound. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Tinnitus WITH hearing loss and vertigo together points to which diagnosis?","choices":["Benign positional vertigo","Acute labyrinthitis","Otitis externa","Ménière disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tinnitus with hearing loss AND vertigo together is Ménière; it is the combination that makes the diagnosis, not any one of the three. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which bacteria are named as the common causes of otitis media?","choices":["Streptococcus pyogenes and Moraxella lacunata","Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus","Aspergillus niger and Candida albicans"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the two organisms named for this finding, and both are respiratory tract commensals that reach the middle ear through the Eustachian tube. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A TENDER thyroid suggests what?","choices":["Thyroiditis","Graves disease","Endemic goitre","A solitary cyst"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diffuse enlargement splits three ways on texture alone: soft is Graves, firm is Hashimoto, and tender is thyroiditis. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which viral infections are listed as causes of SENSORINEURAL hearing loss?","choices":["Rhinovirus and influenza","Respiratory syncytial virus and adenovirus","Herpes simplex and varicella zoster","Rubella and cytomegalovirus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — both damage the inner ear, and both matter most when the exposure was congenital. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What kind of pressure is applied to the bulb during pneumatic otoscopy?","choices":["A series of rapid repeated squeezes","Quick, firm but gentle","Slow and sustained, held for several seconds","As hard as the patient will tolerate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Only a small pressure change is needed; the membrane's movement is slight, and force adds nothing but discomfort. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which three findings together describe the tympanic membrane in acute otitis media?","choices":["Red, retracted, and with the landmarks exaggerated","Pale, thickened and immobile","Amber with visible bubbles","Red, bulging, and with the landmarks lost"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The purulent effusion behind it pushes it outward and obscures the normal anatomy. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which cranial nerves carry referred pain to the ear?","choices":["V, VII, IX and X","III, IV and VI","VIII alone","XI and XII"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Four nerves supply sensation to the ear, which is exactly why disease well away from the ear can present as otalgia. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A tympanic membrane perforation that does NOT extend to the margin is called what?","choices":["Traumatic","Central","Marginal","Attic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The classification is simply by whether the margin is involved, and a marginal perforation is the one that does involve it. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How is the auricle positioned to straighten the ear canal for otoscopy in an adult?","choices":["Press it forward against the cheek","Leave it undisturbed and advance the speculum slowly","Pull it up, back, and away from the head","Pull it down and back"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — all three directions. The canal is not straight, and the view is only as good as the alignment. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Submandibular swelling and erythema with cellulitis of the floor of the mouth. What is it, and what is the danger?","choices":["A peritonsillar abscess — the danger is rupture","A submandibular stone — the danger is infection","Ludwig's angina — it is life threatening because of the AIRWAY","Ludwig's angina — the danger is spread to the mediastinum only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The swelling pushes the tongue and floor of mouth upward and back, and the airway is the reason this cannot wait. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A MULTINODULAR enlarged thyroid carries a risk of malignancy under what circumstance?","choices":["Always, regardless of history","Only if the gland is tender","Only in patients under thirty","When there is a family history"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the multinodular gland is usually a metabolic process, and family history is what raises the concern. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What frequency pattern does presbycusis take?","choices":["Loss confined to the speech range, sparing higher and lower tones","Higher frequency loss","Lower frequency loss","All frequencies equally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is why the complaint is about clarity rather than volume — the high registers carry the consonants. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which chain comes FIRST in the standard sequence for palpating the neck nodes?","choices":["Submental","Deep cervical","Preauricular","Supraclavicular"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The sequence runs preauricular, postauricular, occipital, tonsillar, submandibular, submental, superficial cervical, posterior cervical, deep cervical, supraclavicular — following a route so none is missed. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A raised, shiny, slow-growing lesion on the auricle with visible telangiectasia. What should be suspected?","choices":["A tophus","Basal cell carcinoma","A cutaneous cyst","A keloid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Raised, shiny, telangiectatic and slow growing — and malignant, which is what separates it from the benign lumps in the same list. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Necrotising ulcerative gingivitis is known by which other names?","choices":["Angular cheilitis, and perleche","Aphthous stomatitis, and canker sores","Vincent's angina, and trench mouth","Ludwig's angina, and quinsy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The second name records where it was first described in numbers, in the trenches of the First World War. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"The tongue deviates to one side on protrusion. Which nerve is involved?","choices":["Cranial nerve VII","Cranial nerve XII","Cranial nerve X","Cranial nerve V"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asymmetric protrusion is the hypoglossal sign, and it is why the tongue is checked for midline position. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What are the four Centor criteria?","choices":["Fever, tonsillar exudates, swollen anterior cervical adenopathy, and PRESENCE of cough","Fever, sore throat, headache and myalgia","Fever, trismus, uvular deviation and drooling","Fever, tonsillar exudates, swollen anterior cervical adenopathy, and ABSENCE of cough"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Three are things present and one is a thing absent, which is the part most often misremembered. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Why should dentures be removed during the oral examination?","choices":["To allow the tongue blade to be inserted","To let the patient speak more clearly during the history","To look underneath for ulcers and lesions","To assess the fit of the denture itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A denture hides exactly the mucosa most at risk from chronic irritation. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A DIFFUSELY enlarged, SOFT thyroid. Which condition does that suggest?","choices":["Graves disease","Hashimoto thyroiditis","Thyroiditis","A thyroid cyst"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diffuse and soft is Graves; diffuse and firm is Hashimoto. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which epistaxis history suggests a SYSTEMIC problem rather than a local one?","choices":["Recurrent bleeding, or bleeding and bruising elsewhere on the body","Bleeding from one nostril after digital trauma","Bleeding that follows a recent upper respiratory infection","Bleeding in dry winter air"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. One nosebleed is local until proven otherwise; a pattern of bleeding at other sites changes the question entirely. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"How does the course of CENTRAL vertigo differ in duration from the peripheral causes?","choices":["Always under one minute","Always several hours","Variable, but rarely continuous","Always continuous until treated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the table's wording. It is a useful contrast because the peripheral causes each have a fairly characteristic duration. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A TENDER node and a HARD, FIXED node each suggest what?","choices":["Tender suggests malignancy; hard and fixed suggests inflammation","Both suggest inflammation","Both suggest malignancy","Tender suggests inflammation; hard and fixed suggests malignancy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The two most informative qualities of a node are how it feels and whether it moves. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"At what age do conductive and sensorineural hearing loss typically begin?","choices":["Conductive in childhood and young adulthood up to about forty; sensorineural in middle or later years","Conductive in middle or later years; sensorineural in childhood","Both typically begin after sixty","Both typically begin in childhood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the split is useful because it puts the prior probability in place before you touch a tuning fork. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"The soft palate fails to rise and the uvula deviates to the opposite side. Which nerve is affected?","choices":["Cranial nerve XII — the uvula deviates toward the paralysed side","Cranial nerve IX, with the uvula deviating toward the lesion","Cranial nerve VII, with asymmetric palatal elevation","Cranial nerve X — the uvula deviates AWAY from the paralysed side"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The working side pulls unopposed, so the uvula points away from the lesion. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which three structures are inspected and palpated before the otoscope is picked up?","choices":["The auricles, the mastoid and the tragus","The tympanic membrane, the ossicles and the cochlea","The parotid gland, the temporomandibular joint and the zygomatic arch","The preauricular, postauricular and occipital nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tenderness found here localises disease before anything is put into the canal, and tragal tenderness in particular points to the canal itself. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Cracking and inflammation at the CORNERS of the mouth. What is this called?","choices":["Angular cheilitis","Angioedema","Herpes simplex","Aphthous ulceration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — one of three lip abnormalities to recognise, alongside angioedema and herpes simplex. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Vertigo comes on when the patient rolls onto one side in bed, lasts under a minute, and hearing is unaffected. Which is it?","choices":["Benign positional vertigo","Ménière disease","Vestibular neuronitis","Acoustic neuroma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sudden onset on rolling onto the affected side or tilting the head up, a few seconds to under a minute, hearing not affected, tinnitus absent. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which tuning fork is used for the hearing tests, and under what conditions?","choices":["Any available fork, since only the comparison matters","A 512 hertz fork — the smaller one — in a quiet room","A 128 hertz fork — the larger one — in a quiet room","A 512 hertz fork, with background noise to mask the other ear"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The frequency matters because it sits in the speech range and rings long enough to compare two positions. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 39"}]};
